{"id":"outposts","corpus":"aws","title":"AWS Outposts","sourceUrl":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/outposts/latest/userguide/what-is-outposts.html","license":"AWS Documentation © Amazon Web Services, Inc.","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"AWS Outposts"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Outposts is a fully managed service that extends AWS infrastructure, services, APIs, and tools to customer premises. By providing local access to AWS managed infrastructure, AWS Outposts enables customers to build and run applications on premises using the same programming interfaces as in AWS Regions, while using local compute and storage resources for lower latency and local data processing needs."},{"kind":"p","text":"An Outpost is a pool of AWS compute and storage capacity deployed at a customer site. AWS operates, monitors, and manages this capacity as part of an AWS Region. You can create subnets on your Outpost and specify them when you create AWS resources such as EC2 instances, EBS volumes, ECS clusters, and RDS instances. Instances in Outpost subnets communicate with other instances in the AWS Region using private IP addresses, all within the same VPC."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can't connect an Outpost to another Outpost or Local Zone that is within the same VPC."},{"kind":"p","text":"For more information, see the AWS Outposts product page."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Key concepts"},{"kind":"p","text":"These are the key concepts for AWS Outposts."},{"kind":"li","text":"Outpost site – The customer-managed physical buildings where AWS will install your Outpost. A site must meet the facility, networking, and power requirements for your Outpost."},{"kind":"li","text":"Outpost capacity – Compute and storage resources available on the Outpost. You can view and manage the capacity for your Outpost from the AWS Outposts console. AWS Outposts supports self-service capacity management that you can define at the Outposts level to reconfigure all of the assets in an Outposts or specifically for each individual asset. An Outpost asset can be a single server within an Outposts rack or an Outposts server."},{"kind":"li","text":"Outpost equipment – Physical hardware that provides access to the AWS Outposts service. The hardware includes racks, servers, switches, and cabling owned and managed by AWS."},{"kind":"li","text":"Outposts racks – An Outpost form factor that is an industry-standard 42U rack. Outposts racks include rack-mountable servers, switches, a network patch panel, a power shelf and blank panels."},{"kind":"li","text":"Outposts ACE racks – The Aggregation, Core, Edge (ACE) rack acts as a network aggregation point for multi-rack Outpost deployments. The ACE rack reduces the number of physical networking port and logical interface requirements by providing connectivity between multiple Outpost compute racks in your logical Outposts and your on-premise network. You must install an ACE rack if you have four or more compute racks. If you have less than four compute racks but plan to expand to four or more racks in the future, we recommend that you install an ACE rack at the earliest. For additional information on ACE racks, see Scaling AWS Outposts rack deployments with ACE racks."},{"kind":"li","text":"Outposts servers – An Outpost form factor that is an industry-standard 1U or 2U server, which can be installed in a standard EIA-310D 19 compliant 4 post rack. Outposts servers provide local compute and networking services to sites that have limited space or smaller capacity requirements."},{"kind":"li","text":"Outpost owner – The account owner for the account that places the AWS Outposts order. After AWS engages with the customer, the owner may include additional points of contact. AWS will communicate with the contacts to clarify orders, installation appointments, and hardware maintenance and replacement. Contact AWS Support Center if the contact information changes."},{"kind":"li","text":"Service link – Network route that enables communication between your Outpost and its associated AWS Region. Each Outpost is an extension of an Availability Zone and its associated Region."},{"kind":"li","text":"Local gateway (LGW) – A logical interconnect virtual router that enables communication between an Outposts rack and your on-premises network."},{"kind":"li","text":"Local network interface – A network interface that enables communication from an Outposts server and your on-premises network."},{"kind":"h2","text":"AWS resources on Outposts"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can create the following resources on your Outpost to support low-latency workloads that must run in close proximity to on-premises data and applications:"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Supported AWS services by AWS Region"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Outposts supports AWS services based on the AWS Region in which your Outpost operates. To determine the supported services, view your Region in the respective geographic area:"},{"kind":"h3","text":"North America"},{"kind":"p","text":"The following table indicates AWS Outposts support for AWS services in North America Regions."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Africa"},{"kind":"p","text":"The following table indicates AWS Outposts support for AWS services in Africa Regions."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Asia Pacific"},{"kind":"p","text":"The following table indicates AWS Outposts support for AWS services in Asia Pacific Regions."}],"lemmas":{"outposts":["outpost","outposts"],"fullies":["fullies","fully"],"managed":["managed","manage"],"manageds":["manageds","managed"],"managing":["managing","manage"],"serviced":["serviced","service"],"servicing":["servicing","service"],"extends":["extends","extend"],"extended":["extended","extend"],"extending":["extending","extend"],"infrastructures":["infrastructure","infrastructures"],"services":["service","services"],"apis":["apis","api"],"tools":["tool","tools"],"tooling":["tooling","tool"],"premises":["premise","premises"],"providing":["providing","provide"],"providings":["providings","providing"],"provided":["pr